EXT. ICE-FISHING SHACK VILLAGE - FROZEN LAKE - DUSK

A scatter of tarpaper shacks on a vast white lake, each with a stovepipe breathing smoke. The wind has a knife in it.

HALVARD, sixties, a fisherman in a fur hat, crouches at a hole with a jig. ANNIKA, thirties, his daughter, holds a thermos. RUSLAN, forties, a stranger in a thin jacket, stands in the doorway. TOVA, ten, sits on a bucket. BJORN, thirties, Annika's husband, tends the stove. OLD MARGIT, eighties, wrapped in two blankets, dozes in the corner.

Halvard hauls up a line. Nothing on it. He drops it back. Ruslan watches, shivering. Annika offers him the thermos. He takes it with both hands.

RUSLAN
Thank you.

ANNIKA
Close the door.

RUSLAN
My car is dead.

BJORN
(to Ruslan)
Sit by the stove.

Ruslan sits. Tova stares at his shoes. Margit stirs.

MARGIT
(to nobody)
Ice is thinning.

HALVARD
Margit, sleep.

ANNIKA
Papa, listen.

A groan from the lake, long and deep, like a whale turning. Everyone looks at the floor.

HALVARD
Out. All of us.

ANNIKA
Papa, the ice...

HALVARD
Now!

TOVA
Mama!

ANNIKA ^
Go!
